// THE BASS ENGINE - dev (fully commented) - v13
// The bass-driven sibling to "Ship of Theseus (no voice)". That project's
// whole architecture is kick-driven (kick decided first, songStable, bass
// told to avoid its steps) - it fits techno/house/trance/psy/dubtechno/
// downtempo natively, but is backwards for genres where the bass/808/sub-
// line IS the actual rhythmic and harmonic backbone: kick reinforces or
// drops out, not the other way around. This engine exists for that family.
//
// v1-v2 (as "Trap Engine") were single-genre, trap only. v3 generalizes the
// same proven core into a MODE_PRESETS-driven multi-genre engine, the exact
// same pattern Ship of Theseus itself uses - one shared engine, per-genre
// dial settings, not one file per genre (see that project's own CLAUDE.md
// for why: the shared machinery - seeded hash PRNG, energy/breakdown walk,
// key/scale system, per-voice skeleton+drift+rest, ghost/wander, motif
// generator - is genuinely genre-agnostic and was real work to get right
// once; duplicating it per genre would just be drift risk for no benefit).
// `trap` below is a byte-for-byte port of v2's hardcoded values into preset
// fields - a pure refactor, not a retune. Three presets this engine exists
// for today - trap, dub, dubstep - are built (see the MODE_PRESETS comment
// below for what each needed).
//
// v9 renamed a since-removed fourth genre `dnb` -> `dnbHalftime` after real
// drum & bass research found its v3-v8 comments' framing backwards, and
// v10 added a proper mainstream/dancefloor `dnb` preset alongside it. Both
// are gone as of v11 - not lost, forked out to the standalone "DnB Engine"
// sibling project once a follow-up audit found `dnb`'s own bass pattern was
// being derived FROM the documented kick/breakbeat positions, backwards
// from this engine's bass-leads-kick premise, because this file had no
// mechanism to represent DnB's real kick-leads-breakbeat identity any other
// way. See MODE_PRESETS' own comment block below for the full removal
// note, and "DnB Engine"'s own CLAUDE.md for the fork's full reasoning and
// what changed once DnB had its own file to fix it in properly. This
// engine's job from here is trap/dub/dubstep - if you're tempted to add a
// DnB-lineage genre back in, it belongs in the DnB Engine sibling project,
// not here.
//
// v12 adds a second lead-melody shape: motifStyle ('arch', the original
// generator, or 'arp', new). Researched before building, not assumed -
// arpeggiated leads are a real, separately-citable convention for both
// genres that use motifOn: trap's "arpeggiated and gated synth leads...
// minor triads, 1/16th note" is a documented standard technique, and
// melodic dubstep names "arpeggiated synth motifs" as a defining palette
// element, run continuously and quietly under the track (not restated-
// every-N-bars like this engine's original arch motif) specifically so
// there's still melody once the bass takes over. No citation found for an
// arpeggiated lead in dub, which doesn't use motifOn anyway (skank already
// fills dub's harmonic-texture role) - left alone rather than guessed.
// motifStyle:'arp' reuses the EXISTING chord-degree/scale-root machinery
// motifPattern() already shared with bass808Pattern() via chordDegreeAt() -
// that coupling (lead and bass agreeing on the current local chord root)
// predates this change; what's new is the arpeggio CONTOUR itself
// (motifArpDegAt()) and continuous 16th-note gating, not the root-sync.
// User's own call on the contour: seeded-shuffle random order through the
// chord tones (a real, cited arpeggiator mode) rather than the also-cited
// up-only/up-down modes - reshuffled every full pass through the triad, not
// re-rolled note-by-note, so it can't fall into the same "unbounded tail,
// most-likely-outcome-is-degenerate" shape this project's Working Style
// section already warns about.
//
// Companion file: "Bass Engine live.js" - terse twin, same values/logic, `// set:`
// hints only, what actually gets pasted into Strudel. Edit here first,
// mirror into the live file, bump both versions, verify parity - same
// discipline the Ship of Theseus project uses (see its CLAUDE.md).
// ============================================================
//  Everything you touch mid-set is in this block. Nothing below it.
// ============================================================

const midi = 'IAC Driver Bus 1';   // your MIDI port

// -- the two you'll actually reach for -------------------------
const SEED     = 7;      // track change. New 808 line, new roll placement, new kit.
const OPEN_KEY = '6m';   // key, Camelot notation. 6m = D minor.

// -- tempo --------------------------------------------------------
//   trap     130-170 (commonly ~140-150, felt half-time - snare/clap on
//            beat 3, not the 2-and-4 backbeat, same convention Ship of
//            Theseus already validated for dubstep)
//   dub      60-90 (classic one-drop range; not yet set below - flagged,
//            not fixed here, per the user's own call to handle tempo)
//   dubstep  138-142 (canonical dubstep tempo, felt half-time like trap -
//            same range Ship of Theseus's own now-retired dubstep mode
//            validated)
//   (dnb/dnbHalftime tempo guidance moved with them to "DnB Engine" in v11)
const BPM = 140;
const BPM_RAMP_SECONDS = 8;

if (typeof setcpm !== 'undefined') {
  const _tempo = (globalThis.__bassEngineTempo = globalThis.__bassEngineTempo || { bpm: undefined, timer: null });
  if (_tempo.timer) { clearInterval(_tempo.timer); _tempo.timer = null; }
  if (_tempo.bpm === undefined || BPM_RAMP_SECONDS <= 0 || _tempo.bpm === BPM) {
    _tempo.bpm = BPM;
    setcpm(BPM / 4);
  } else {
    const fromBpm = _tempo.bpm, toBpm = BPM, stepMs = 100;
    const steps = Math.max(1, Math.round(BPM_RAMP_SECONDS * 1000 / stepMs));
    let step = 0;
    _tempo.timer = setInterval(() => {
      step += 1;
      const t = Math.min(1, step / steps);
      _tempo.bpm = fromBpm + (toBpm - fromBpm) * t;
      setcpm(_tempo.bpm / 4);
      if (t >= 1) { clearInterval(_tempo.timer); _tempo.timer = null; }
    }, stepMs);
  }
}

// -- mode ---------------------------------------------------------
//   trap/dub/dubstep - all three built out, see the MODE_PRESETS comment
//   block below for what each needed and its sourcing.
const MODE = 'trap';

// Nudge ONE field away from the chosen mode's preset without switching
// modes. Field names match the keys in MODE_PRESETS below.
const MODE_OVERRIDES = {};

// ============================================================
//  Below here is the engine. You should not need to touch it.
// ============================================================

const drumChan = 1, bass808Chan = 2, leadChan = 3, skankChan = 4;

const STEPS = 16;       // bar grid for kick/snare/808/melody
const HAT_STEPS = 32;   // hats run on a FINER grid - a roll needs room to
                         // pack more than 16 hits/bar into a single beat.
                         // Every other voice stays on the coarser 16-grid.
const BARS_PER_PHRASE = 4;

const sig = (typeof signal !== 'undefined') ? signal : (f) => time.fmap(f);

// ---- reused verbatim from Ship of Theseus: seeded hash PRNG, its growing-
// memoized-chain helper, and the (step,bar) decomposition every per-16th
// signal callback needs. None of this is genre-specific. ----
function rnd(kind, a, b) {
  let h = (kind * 374761393 + a * 668265263 + b * 2246822519 + SEED * 3266489917) | 0;
  h = Math.imul(h ^ (h >>> 13), 1274126177);
  h = Math.imul(h ^ (h >>> 16), 2246822507);
  h = h ^ (h >>> 15);
  return (h >>> 0) / 4294967296;
}
function growChain(arr, i, step) {
  if (!(i >= 0)) i = 0;
  i = Math.min(i, arr.length + 4096);
  while (arr.length <= i) arr.push(step(arr));
  return arr[i];
}
// steps param lets the hat voice use its own finer grid (HAT_STEPS) while
// everything else uses the default (STEPS).
const stepBar = (t, steps = STEPS) => [Math.floor(t * steps) % steps, Math.floor(t)];

function weightedChoice(kind, vi, step, sec, choices) {
  const r = rnd(kind, vi * 997 + step, sec);
  let acc = 0;
  for (const c of choices) { acc += c.w; if (r < acc) return c; }
  return choices[0];
}

// ============================================================
//  MODE_PRESETS - the actual per-genre bundle. See the field-by-field
//  sourcing comment on MODE_PRESETS.trap below for what everything means
//  and where it came from.
//
//  HONEST STATE (v11): all three genres this engine exists for - trap,
//  dub, dubstep - are built. See each preset's own comments below for full
//  sourcing; the summary here is what each needed beyond a mechanical
//  preset port.
//
//  dub needed two real new mechanisms: a skank voice (channel 4,
//  skankPattern() below - off-beat muted chord chops, the reggae/dub
//  guitar's signature move, same idea as Ship of Theseus's own now-retired
//  skank voice) and a kickDropsOut flag (kick now CONDITIONALLY survives a
//  breakdown instead of always dropping with the 808 - trap's "kick only
//  exists to reinforce the 808, so it drops when the 808 does" isn't
//  universal; dub's one-drop kick+snare unit keeps pulsing through a
//  breakdown per Ship of Theseus's own retired dub research: "kick stays,
//  bass still drops normally"). kickReinforce itself did NOT need
//  replacing - one-drop's kick+bass togetherness on beat 3 fits the
//  existing "kick reinforces 808 hits" mechanism fine once the 808 pattern
//  has a real, dominant hit AT beat 3 to lock onto.
//
//  dubstep needed nothing new - preset values only, leaning on the same
//  halftime-snare and kick-reinforces-bass machinery trap and dub already
//  proved, with dub's kickDropsOut:true flipped back to match trap's
//  reasoning instead (dubstep's build/drop structure strips to atmosphere,
//  not a one-drop kick that stays).
//
//  dnb/dnbHalftime (mainstream DnB and Halftime, v9-v10) lived here
//  through v10 - see this file's own prior versions or git history for the
//  full presets. REMOVED in v11: a follow-up audit found `dnb`'s bass
//  pattern was being derived FROM the documented kick/breakbeat positions,
//  backwards from this engine's bass-leads-kick premise, because this file
//  had no mechanism to represent DnB's real kick-leads-breakbeat identity
//  any other way. `dnbHalftime` genuinely does fit this engine's
//  architecture (same as trap/dubstep) but moved anyway, by explicit user
//  choice, so the whole DnB-engine family stays together in its own proper
//  home. Both now live in the standalone "DnB Engine" sibling project,
//  which gives `dnb` a real kick-leads mechanism instead of forcing it
//  through this file's bass-leads-only shape - see that project's own
//  CLAUDE.md for the fork's full reasoning.
// ============================================================
const MODE_PRESETS = {
  // trap - ported directly from v2's hardcoded constants, one field per
  // constant, same values, same logic. Every comment below is carried over
  // from where that value originally lived; nothing was retuned in this
  // port.
  trap: {
    // -- 808 (the genre's actual backbone - see bass808Pattern() below) --
    // Hand-designed, NOT a documented statistical convention - unlike, say,
    // the Amen break (which has real canonical breakpoints), trap 808
    // patterns vary enormously track to track and there is no single
    // citable "the" trap 808 rhythm. This is one idiomatically syncopated
    // shape (hit on 1, a quick pickup on the "e" of 1, a syncopated run
    // around beats 2-3, an anticipation into the next bar) - refine by ear
    // against a real reference track, the same honesty Ship of Theseus
    // asks for when no rigorous evidence exists.
    bass808Pattern: [0, 3, 7, 10, 13],
    bass808Octave: 36,
    bass808Drift: 0.35,
    bass808Rest: 0.10,
    // True 808 slide/glide (continuous pitch-bend between notes) is real
    // trap production practice but NOT attempted here - it would need
    // Strudel's own pitch-bend/`.slide()`-equivalent verified live first
    // (the same "flag the assumption, don't fake it" stance Ship of
    // Theseus took with `.mask()`). What's here instead: note CHOICES
    // weighted toward stepwise-connected motion (root/5th, an octave-down
    // sub hit, a b7 passing tone) rather than random jumps - an honest
    // approximation of the FEEL, not the actual glide.
    bass808Choices: [
      { idx: 0, oct: 0,  w: 0.70 }, // root - the anchor
      { idx: 4, oct: 0,  w: 0.18 }, // 5th - the most common connecting motion
      { idx: 0, oct: -1, w: 0.07 }, // octave down - the classic sub-drop hit
      { idx: 6, oct: -1, w: 0.05 }, // b7 down an octave - dark passing tone
    ],
    // "808 variation" phrase-ending operation - bias toward the more
    // unstable/dramatic choices (octave-drop, b7) right on the phrase's
    // last bar, a lightweight flourish that reuses weightedChoice() with a
    // different table rather than a new mechanism.
    bass808ChoicesEnd: [
      { idx: 0, oct: 0,  w: 0.40 },
      { idx: 4, oct: 0,  w: 0.15 },
      { idx: 0, oct: -1, w: 0.30 },
      { idx: 6, oct: -1, w: 0.15 },
    ],

    // -- kick (follows the 808, does not lead it - see kickPattern() below) --
    // Reversed from Ship of Theseus's whole-project convention on purpose:
    // there, kick is decided first and bass avoids its steps. Here the 808
    // is decided first, and the kick's own positions are a SUBSET of the
    // 808's - "does this 808 hit get reinforced by a kick" is itself a
    // stable, slowly-drifting per-song choice (same skeleton mechanism as
    // everything else), not re-rolled every bar. kickReinforce is the
    // fraction of 808 hits that get a simultaneous kick - trap kicks are
    // usually short/tight and don't double every 808 hit (that would just
    // read as a louder 808, not a kick). Reasonable middle estimate, not a
    // hard citation, same as bass808Pattern above.
    kickReinforce: 0.6,
    // low - which 808 hits get doubled shouldn't flip constantly, or the
    // kick stops reading as "attached to" specific 808 hits at all.
    kickReinforceDrift: 0.15,
    // rare extra syncopated kick in an 808 GAP - kept low, trap kicks are
    // sparse, not busy.
    kickGhost: 0.06,
    // beat 1 as the kick's own anchor point is close to a universal drum-
    // programming convention, independent of genre - treating it as just
    // another 808-reinforce position undersells it, especially since
    // kickReinforceDrift can let that mechanism wander away from step 0
    // for stretches. This is a SEPARATE, independent, per-song stable roll
    // (not tied to whether the 808 happens to hit step 0 at all, so it
    // holds even if bass808Pattern changes). High but not absolute,
    // matching "high probability" rather than "always."
    kickBeat1Chance: 0.85,
    // "kick variation" phrase-ending operation - extra syncopated ghost
    // kicks leading into the phrase boundary.
    kickFillBoost: 2.5,
    // trap's whole identity is 808-and-kick-as-one-instrument - when the
    // 808 drops out for a breakdown, the kick (which exists only to
    // reinforce it) should too. See dub's own kickDropsOut for the
    // opposite case and why.
    kickDropsOut: true,

    // -- snare/clap (half-time, beat 3 - see snarePattern() below) --
    // Same halftime convention Ship of Theseus already validated for
    // dubstep (CLAP_PATTERNS.halftime = [8]) - both genres share the same
    // "written fast, felt half-time" relationship between tempo and pulse.
    snarePattern: [8],
    snareDrift: 0.30,
    snareRest: 0.15,
    snareGhost: 0.30,
    // "snare fill" phrase-ending operation - more ghost hits leading into
    // the phrase boundary, reusing the existing ghost mechanism rather
    // than a separate roll.
    snareFillBoost: 2.2,
    // A researched trap grammar reference (user-supplied) is explicit that
    // timing here should be "100% quantization by default, swing 0,
    // microtiming/humanization OPTIONAL, and DO NOT enforce fixed
    // millisecond offsets." An earlier version had this at a nonzero
    // constant applied via .late() to every snare hit - that's literally a
    // fixed millisecond offset, exactly what the rule says not to do (a
    // constant nudge reads as mechanical over a long set - real "human"
    // timing variation is a distribution, not a repeated number). Kept at
    // 0 - the underlying "laid-back snare" production knowledge was real,
    // but a single constant push was the wrong SHAPE to express it in, not
    // just the wrong number. True per-hit-varying humanization would need
    // Strudel's .late()/.early() verified to accept a per-event signal
    // rather than only a constant - not attempted, same "flag the
    // assumption, don't fake it" stance as the 808 glide above.
    snareNudgeMs: 0,

    // -- hats (the signature move - see hatPattern() below) --
    // Trap's identifying gesture isn't a density dial, it's a
    // probabilistic BURST: a beat's normal 16th-note pace suddenly packs
    // in extra hits (a "roll"), then drops back. rollOn is the on/off -
    // false for genres that don't want this mechanism at all (same
    // pattern as Ship of Theseus's hatRollOn, which this was ported FROM).
    rollOn: true,
    hatBaseSubsteps: [0, 2, 4, 6],  // plain straight 16ths within a beat's 8 sub-steps
    rollBaseChance: 0.12,            // ~1 roll every ~8 beats outside phrase-end boosts
    rollPhraseEndMult: 2.5,          // far more likely as a fill into a phrase boundary
    rollLengths: [{ n: 6, w: 0.45 }, { n: 8, w: 0.55 }],
    // Base (resting) pattern is plain straight 16ths, NOT swung - real trap
    // hats are typically dead straight; the bounce comes from roll
    // placement and velocity shaping, not timing swing.
    // swingPct:50 is a true no-op (SWING = (50-50)/50 = 0, withSwing()
    // below skips the .swingBy() call entirely rather than calling it with
    // a zero amount) - deliberately straight, not "no mechanism exists" -
    // v4 added a real withSwing()/SWING_GRID mechanism (ported directly
    // from Ship of Theseus's own, already-proven one) once dub needed it
    // for real. See MODE_PRESETS.dub's own swingPct for the mode that
    // actually uses it.
    swingPct: 50,
    swingGrid: 8,

    // -- harmony --
    // Trap is overwhelmingly minor-key, which scaleMode:'natural' still
    // defaults to - but a researched trap grammar reference (user-
    // supplied) specifically calls out Phrygian as a real available
    // harmonic system, and that one IS well-documented (dark-trap/drill
    // melodic runs using the Phrygian b2), unlike harmonic minor (no case
    // found for that one). phrygianChance decides which minor FLAVOR a
    // song uses, a separate axis from the key's own major/relative-minor
    // relationship - decided once per song (same "stable per song"
    // pattern as everything else that shouldn't flip mid-track), moderate
    // chance since natural minor is still the more common trap default.
    scaleMode: 'natural',
    phrygianChance: 0.35,
    // modest - trap has no strong "key-lift into the drop" convention the
    // way trance does (that's an EDM technique); no citation found either
    // way, kept low on purpose.
    keyMoveChance: 0.08,
    // Harmony here is short repeating progressions (single-chord/pedal, a
    // 2-chord loop, or a 3-4 chord loop), a genuinely different thing from
    // the slow, multi-minute key-drift mechanism above (long-term key
    // movement, not a bar-to-bar progression within a phrase). One chord
    // per bar, one loop template chosen per song (stable for the whole
    // track, matching how a real trap beat's progression usually doesn't
    // change mid-song). Templates and weights are a reasonable estimate of
    // relative frequency, not a citation - i-VI and i-VII are the most
    // commonly cited trap/pop-minor progressions, pedal/single-chord is
    // also common (especially haunting, 808-led beats), i-iv and the
    // 4-chord loop are real but less dominant. Degrees: 0=i, 5=VI, 6=VII,
    // 3=iv - all diatonic to whatever scale/key is already active.
    chordLoopTemplates: [
      { degs: [0],          w: 0.30 }, // single-chord/pedal
      { degs: [0, 5],       w: 0.25 }, // i - VI
      { degs: [0, 6],       w: 0.20 }, // i - VII
      { degs: [0, 3],       w: 0.15 }, // i - iv
      { degs: [0, 5, 6, 5], w: 0.10 }, // i - VI - VII - VI
    ],
    chordLoopBarLen: 1, // bars per chord - one change per bar is typical trap pacing

    // -- melody (sparse, minimal lead - see motifPattern() below) --
    // Trap melodic leads (bell/pluck/piano) are famously minimal, often
    // just 2-4 notes, present for a couple of bars then silent for several
    // more - motifCycle:4 (a 2-bar phrase that only restates every 4 bars,
    // so it's silent half the time) and the [2,4] note range reflect that
    // directly.
    motifOn: true,
    // 'arp' (v12): "arpeggiated and gated synth leads... minor triads and
    // 1/16th note LFOs" is cited as a standard trap lead technique -
    // genuinely different from the sparse arch-shaped hook this engine
    // originally had, not a retune of it. See motifArpDegAt() below.
    motifStyle: 'arp',
    // v13 correction (see motifArpDegAt()'s own comment for the full
    // "sounds off" story): 8th notes, not literal continuous 16ths - tried
    // at 16ths first per the citation above, reported too fast/machine-
    // gun-like by ear, backed off while staying denser than dubstep's
    // sparser motifArpDiv:4 (matches the relative gap the two genres'
    // sourcing actually supports).
    motifArpDiv: 2,
    // No citation prefers one arpeggiator mode over another for trap
    // specifically - hand-designed weighting, mostly the two fixed/
    // repeating shapes (up, updown) that actually read as a phrase, random
    // kept as a minority accent rather than the constant mode v12 had.
    motifArpOrders: [
      { order: 'up',     w: 0.40 },
      { order: 'updown', w: 0.45 },
      { order: 'random', w: 0.15 },
    ],
    motifCycle: 4,
    motifNoteRange: [2, 4],
    motifOctave: 72, // bell/pluck register, well clear of the 808
    motifClip: 0.55, // short and plucky, not sustained

    // -- skank (off for trap - see dub for the mode that actually uses this) --
    skankOn: false,
    skankPattern: [2, 6, 10, 14],
    skankDrift: 0.25,
    skankRest: 0.15,
    skankGhost: 0,
    skankOctave: 67,
    skankClip: 0.25,

    // -- arrangement --
    // trap's hook/beat-switch cadence is nowhere near as rigorously
    // documented as EDM breakdown timing (no canonical "trap breakdown
    // length" the way techno/trance have researched conventions) - kept at
    // a moderate default consistent with Ship of Theseus's own least-
    // committal mode ("custom"), not a hard citation.
    dropPatience: 15,
    dropBars: 6,
    // trap tracks commonly run 2.5-4 minutes, noticeably shorter than the
    // 6-8 minute DJ-tool length of most house/techno - a genuinely well-
    // documented genre convention (streaming-era hip-hop/trap runtimes),
    // unlike dropPatience/dropBars above.
    songMinutes: 3.2,
    sectionSeconds: 32, // ~8 bars at this tempo - one verse/hook length
    sparse: 1.1,
  },

  // dub - reggae/dub's one-drop: nothing on beat 1, kick+snare land TOGETHER
  // on beat 3 - that togetherness IS the genre's name ("dropping" the beat
  // on 3). Renamed from "reggae" on purpose, same reasoning Ship of
  // Theseus's own (now-retired) dub mode used: dub versions are
  // instrumental remixes, which is exactly what a MIDI rhythm-section
  // engine can actually produce - drum+bass+skank, no vocal, no claim to
  // more than that.
  dub: {
    // Deliberately sparse and deliberately silent on beat 1 (step 0) - real
    // one-drop bass leaves the same space the drums do. Two hits: the drop
    // itself (step 8, beat 3 - where kick+snare also land) and an
    // anticipation into the next bar (step 14, the "and" of 4, a classic
    // reggae bass turnaround). This is a genuinely different design
    // approach from trap's own bass808Pattern (5 hits, syncopated,
    // starting on beat 1) for a specific mechanical reason, not just genre
    // flavor: kickReinforce below is a FLAT rate applied to every
    // bass808Pattern hit position equally - there's no per-position
    // weighting in this engine yet. A very sparse pattern is what lets
    // one-drop's "everything centers on beat 3" character come through
    // cleanly with the existing flat-rate mechanism, rather than needing a
    // new per-position-weighted one. If a future mode wants MORE bass
    // movement with kick reinforcement concentrated on just one of those
    // hits, that's the point where this mechanism would need extending.
    bass808Pattern: [8, 14],
    bass808Octave: 36,
    // Low - dub's rhythm section ("riddim") is famously hypnotic and
    // repetitive; a real dub mix restates the same riddim for many minutes
    // with only slow variation, unlike trap's more restless 808 lines.
    bass808Drift: 0.15,
    // Low - one-drop bass is disciplined and reliable, not sparse-random;
    // it's supposed to be there every time.
    bass808Rest: 0.05,
    // Root-and-fifth, not root-and-occasional-everything-else - directly
    // reused from Ship of Theseus's own (now-retired) BASS_CHOICES_DUBBY,
    // real reasoning that still applies: the 5th gets real harmonic weight
    // here, unlike trap's more root-anchored, rarely-wandering line.
    bass808Choices: [
      { idx: 0, oct: 0,  w: 0.55 },
      { idx: 4, oct: 0,  w: 0.30 },
      { idx: 0, oct: 1,  w: 0.10 },
      { idx: 2, oct: 0,  w: 0.05 },
    ],
    // Dub doesn't have trap's "dramatic flourish right before the phrase
    // boundary" convention - no citation found for one, so no separate
    // end-of-phrase table; same choices used throughout, which is itself a
    // deliberate decision, not an oversight.
    bass808ChoicesEnd: [
      { idx: 0, oct: 0,  w: 0.55 },
      { idx: 4, oct: 0,  w: 0.30 },
      { idx: 0, oct: 1,  w: 0.10 },
      { idx: 2, oct: 0,  w: 0.05 },
    ],

    // High and stable - one-drop's whole identity depends on kick and bass
    // reliably landing together on the same hits (there are only two to
    // reinforce here, so "high" reads as "dependable," not "busy").
    kickReinforce: 0.85,
